1	// The executor (tasks-dispatch DESIGN §6) — a pull worker over the queue. Each
2	// iteration: pick the next ready item, claim it by a verified lease, re-evaluate
3	// the precondition (the scheduler run asked once at the anchor; a chained stage
4	// re-derives world state rather than trusting a verdict passed forward), then on
5	// a go run code-work and either converge (agentless) or hand off to an agent
6	// session; on a no-go close the item with the reason on record — the next
7	// occurrence is the scheduler run's ask at the task's next anchor (#1115).
8	//
9	// An executor's whole interface is issue read/write plus the repo at HEAD, which
10	// is what makes it platform-agnostic: the reference deployment is a job in the
11	// vendored workflows (the scheduler run's post-scheduler run drain, and a `labeled`-event run for
12	// latency), but a runner anywhere with an issues-scope token qualifies. Nothing
13	// enumerates executors; identity is self-declared in the claim comment.
14	//
15	// The pure decisions live at the top and test with fixtures; the shell below is
16	// the GitHub/code-work/invocation I/O around them.
